# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Taurus Go — environment secrets (example)
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` is gitignored.
# JWT_SECRET has NO default any more: a service that cannot resolve it will
# FAIL TO START. That is deliberate (H3) -- the old default was committed to
# git, so a silent fallback would mean running on a known-compromised secret.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# JWT signing secret — shared by all services (HS256). Use >= 32 random bytes.
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=

# Postgres password (employee / gamification / project services)
DB_PASSWORD=

# ─── Windows: set once, persists for every new terminal ───
#   $secret = (Get-Content .env | Where-Object { $_ -match '^JWT_SECRET=' }) -replace '^JWT_SECRET=',''
#   [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('JWT_SECRET', $secret, 'User')
#   # then OPEN A NEW TERMINAL (existing ones keep the old environment)
#
# ─── Windows: current terminal only ───
#   $env:JWT_SECRET = "your-strong-secret"
#
# ─── Linux/Mac ───
#   export JWT_SECRET="your-strong-secret"
#   export DB_PASSWORD="your-db-password"
#
# NOTES
#   - All services must share the SAME JWT_SECRET (tokens are cross-service).
#   - Rotating JWT_SECRET invalidates every issued token: all users get logged
#     out once and must sign in again. That is expected.
#   - Still to rotate (see SECURITY-AUDIT.md H3): DB_PASSWORD, RabbitMQ
#     guest/guest, and config-server's ENCRYPT_KEY (which the {cipher} values
#     in config-repo depend on).
